<% content_for :title, "A record-backed editor · yrby" %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <script type="module" src="<%= busted "/document.js" %>"></script>
<% end %>
<div class="docs-body max-w-3xl">
  <p class="text-sm text-accent">Working quickstart</p>
  <h1>A record-backed editor</h1>
  <p>Open this page in two windows and type in either one. Rails stores the
    shared document; the panel below reads it back in Ruby.</p>
  <p>This scratchpad is shared by everyone visiting this example. Its content
    expires after a day without activity. Use sample text.</p>
</div>
<p id="document-status" role="status" class="my-4 text-sm text-zinc-400">Connecting…</p>
<template id="document-template">
<%= collaborative_document_tag @document, :body, id: "document-editor", class: "block rounded-lg border border-zinc-700 bg-zinc-900 p-3" do %>
  <div data-editor-mount aria-label="Shared document"></div>
<% end %>
</template>
<div id="document-container"></div>
<section class="docs-body mt-8 max-w-3xl">
  <h2>Read back from Rails</h2>
  <button id="read-document" class="rounded border border-zinc-600 px-3 py-2 text-sm">Read saved text</button>
  <pre id="stored-document" aria-live="polite">Select “Read saved text” after editing.</pre>
  <%= code_block "ruby", "doc = @document.collaborative_document(:body).doc\ndoc.read_text(\"content\")" %>
  <h2>The integration</h2>
  <p>The page renders <code>collaborative_document_tag</code>. The element
    attaches CodeMirror after sync and releases the editor through its abort
    signal. The document session owns delivery independently of the editor.</p>
  <p>This runs the upcoming client built from the same checkout as the gems.
    The site wraps the shipped channel with its public-demo limits; your
    application supplies its own authorization before rendering the tag.</p>
  <p><a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a> ·
    <a href="/docs/javascript-client">Document lifecycle and recovery</a></p>
</section>
